# Nginx upload body size (VPS / self-hosted)

When an app behind nginx rejects uploads with HTTP **413**, or the UI shows a "file too large" message while the same file works locally:

1. Assume nginx first. Default `client_max_body_size` is **1MB**.
2. App-level limits (e.g. 6MB) do not matter if nginx rejects earlier. The request never reaches Node/Next/pm2.
3. Symptom: no upload logs in the app process; nginx error log may show `client intended to send too large body`.

## Fix

In the site's nginx `server` (or relevant `location`) block:

```nginx
client_max_body_size 10M;
```

Then:

```bash
sudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx
```

Set the value above the app's real max upload size (with a little headroom).

## Checklist for new / other apps on the same VPS

- [ ] Add `client_max_body_size` whenever the app accepts file uploads
- [ ] Do not rely on local-only testing; local has no nginx limit
- [ ] iPhone/Safari often sends larger JPEGs than the Photos app size suggests
- [ ] Confirm with `/var/log/nginx/error.log` if uploads still 413 after deploy

## bananaai

- Site config: `/etc/nginx/sites-available/bananaai`
- App listens on `127.0.0.1:3004`
- App max image size is 6MB; nginx should stay at least `10M` for images
- Seedance 2.5 reference videos can be up to **200MB**; set `client_max_body_size 200M` on `/api/upload/video` (or the whole server block) or 200MB refs will 413 before Node
- Postponed rebrand target: maverickai.ir / MaverickAI

# Nginx proxy timeouts (long generation / ffmpeg)

When an API returns HTML **504 Gateway Time-out** (client may show `Unexpected token '<'`) while Node/pm2 is still working:

1. Assume nginx `proxy_read_timeout` first. Default is often **60s**.
2. Long routes (explainer stitch, TTS, ffmpeg mux) can exceed 60s even when the app `maxDuration` is 300.
3. Symptom: browser gets nginx HTML; app logs may still show the job finishing later.

## Fix

In the bananaai `location` that proxies to Node (or the `server` block):

```nginx
proxy_connect_timeout 60s;
proxy_send_timeout 300s;
proxy_read_timeout 300s;
```

Then:

```bash
sudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx
```

Explainer stitch should return a task id quickly and finish in the background; keep these timeouts high anyway for audio and other long POSTs.
